<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="fructose 2,6-bisphosphate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY66-423" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-535 &quot;Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate&quot;| is a potent allosteric regulator with dual simultaneous functions. It is an activator of |FRAME: HS06881-MONOMER &quot;phosphofructokinase&quot;| and an inhibitor of |FRAME: CPLX66-505 &quot;fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase&quot;|, the key rate-limiting enzymes of glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, respectively |CITS: [1657152][8393580][7574501]|. In plants |FRAME: CPD-535| is a key regulator of primary carbohydrate metabolism in photosynthetic tissues |CITS: [10899575]|. An increased concentration of intracellular |FRAME: CPD-535| increases glycolytic flux, while at low concentrations of the metabolite, there is a net increase in gluconeogenic flux |CITS: [12379646]|. The metabolic role of |FRAME: CPD-535| has great potential for therapeutic intervention in type II diabetes |CITS: [11165514]|. &lt;b&gt;About this Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Mammals and plants have bifunctional 6-phosphofructo-2-kinase/fructose-2,6-bisphosphatases (PFK/FBPases) that catalyze both the synthesis and degradation of |FRAME: CPD-535| due to two independent catalytic domains in a single polypeptide chain. The kinase domain is localized within the amino-terminal part of the enzyme, and the bisphosphatase domain is in the carboxy-terminal region |CITS: [16839880]|.|FRAME: HS01277-MONOMER &quot;TIGAR&quot;|, a novel p53-inducible protein, only catalyzes the dephosphorylation of |FRAME: CPD-535|. In fungi two different enzymes catalyze the two functions. It has been suggested that the primary role of the enzyme is protection against oxidative stress |CITS: [16839880]|. It increases flux through gluconeogenesis, which increases the isomerization of |FRAME: FRUCTOSE-6P| to |FRAME: D-glucopyranose-6-phosphate|. The latter metabolite is diverted into the |FRAME: PENTOSE-P-PWY| resulting in the generation of |FRAME: NADPH| required for |FRAME: PWY-4081| and |FRAME: GLUT-REDOX-PWY| and production of |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE|, an important anti-oxidant.</Description>
